WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:07.286
Over three sittings you build one program: a quote service.

2
00:00:07.286 --> 00:00:15.696
A client sends a line like 7 EURUSD, and within 300 milliseconds it gets the best price from three providers.

3
00:00:15.867 --> 00:00:17.294
Here it is, finished.

4
00:00:17.294 --> 00:00:19.333
A Netty server reads the line.

5
00:00:19.333 --> 00:00:26.809
A coroutine service asks Northbank, Eastgate and Fjord at once, and the best price goes back with the same id.

6
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.000
You build it from the outside in.

7
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:32.880
Each part comes from an earlier lesson, named under the diagram.

8
00:00:33.067 --> 00:00:35.573
Sitting 1, in Java, is the Netty shell.

9
00:00:35.573 --> 00:00:41.035
One event-loop thread runs every handler of its channels, so the handler never waits.

10
00:00:41.035 --> 00:00:43.156
It hands the future back at once.

11
00:00:43.333 --> 00:00:48.567
The reply is written later, back on the same loop, so the handler's state needs no lock.

12
00:00:48.733 --> 00:00:56.207
Lines are capped at 64 bytes, and the loop reads only while the client keeps up and fewer than 16 requests wait.

13
00:00:56.933 --> 00:00:59.533
Sitting 2 writes the service in Kotlin.

14
00:00:59.533 --> 00:01:05.331
Three async calls start together, so a request costs the slowest provider, not the sum.

15
00:01:05.467 --> 00:01:11.020
Each call gets the deadline with withTimeoutOrNull, so a late provider just drops out.

16
00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:15.468
In a supervisorScope, a provider that fails, fails alone.

17
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:20.201
When nobody answers, the last good price goes out, marked stale.

18
00:01:20.201 --> 00:01:21.645
That rule is new here.

19
00:01:22.133 --> 00:01:25.577
And scope.future gives the Java handler its future.

20
00:01:25.577 --> 00:01:29.763
When a client hangs up, cancelling it cancels all three calls.

21
00:01:30.467 --> 00:01:33.568
Sitting 3, still Kotlin, holds it up under load.

22
00:01:33.568 --> 00:01:38.672
Each provider gets its own bulkhead, sized from its contract with Little's law.

23
00:01:38.867 --> 00:01:42.003
Only idempotent calls are retried, with jitter.

24
00:01:42.003 --> 00:01:44.405
Fjord's firm quotes are called once.

25
00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:51.327
Northbank's slow tail gets a hedge: a second call after 80 milliseconds, and the first answer wins.

26
00:01:51.467 --> 00:01:58.556
At the door, admission turns away what it can't answer in time with ERR busy, and shutdown stops everything in order.

27
00:01:59.267 --> 00:02:01.134
All of it rests on one rule.

28
00:02:01.134 --> 00:02:02.935
The event loop never waits.

29
00:02:02.935 --> 00:02:06.604
The waiting happens in coroutines, inside the deadline.
